  "decision": "Zero or more package body elements, exactly as SysML 8.2.2.5.1 states it. This is the start symbol of the SysML grammar: a .sysml file is a sequence of package members, element filters, aliases and imports with no enclosing declaration, and an empty file is a valid root namespace.",
  "notes": "THIS IS THE SYSML VARIANT (ADR-0014). KerML states RootNamespace with a different body, NamespaceBodyElement*, which is not a defect on either side: SysML's root admits package members and element filters where KerML's admits namespace members. That variant is a separate unit and is not consulted here.\n\nThe clause and SysML.xtext agree. The Xtext's `{SysML::Namespace}` is an object-creation action, so an empty file still yields a Namespace; it is abstract-syntax construction and is not carried into the rule.\n\nFOR A PARSER AUTHOR: the existing sv2-syntax parser already implements this body as its root, and applies it to .kerml files too. Under ADR-0014 that is correct for .sysml and not for .kerml, whose root is the KerML variant.\n\nNO KEYWORD AND NULLABLE, so nothing about a file's first token identifies it as a root namespace. It is identified by being the whole file.",
